            "Staurolite is a hydroxylated iron aluminium silicate. It forms in medium-grade regional metamorphism, when aluminium- and iron-rich clays and shales are buried and heated during mountain building; it crystallises in mica schists with almandine garnet, kyanite and muscovite. Its crystals often interpenetrate in cross-shaped twins, at right angles (Greek cross) or at 60° (St Andrew's cross), hence its Greek name “stauros”, cross. In Brittany these “cross stones” have been gathered for centuries as devotional objects.",
            "Monoclinic (pseudo-orthorhombic) system. Stubby reddish-brown to brownish-black prisms, often twinned in crosses; rough surfaces. Distinct cleavage; uneven to subconchoidal fracture. Hardness 7 to 7.5; specific gravity 3.74 to 3.83; vitreous to resinous lustre, dull on weathered crystals; opaque, rarely translucent; refractive index 1.736 to 1.762. Rare transparent fragments yield tiny faceted collector stones; crosses are worn as they are as pendants, uncut. Many “crosses” sold to tourists are moulded imitations: a genuine staurolite is hard, heavy and shows crystal faces.",
            "France: Baud (Morbihan, Brittany), the chapel and heath of Coëtdan, famous crosses taken from weathered schists, and Coray (Finistère). United States: Fannin County (Georgia, Blue Ridge), the “fairy crosses” of Georgia and Virginia. Russia: the Kola Peninsula (Keivy mountains), large crystals and twins. Also Switzerland (Ticino, Pizzo Forno, crystals on kyanite), Brazil, Scotland and Spain (Galicia).",
